  "footnotes": [
    "Neo4j conference keynote (~10:50), delivered by an unnamed speaker who identifies as a Neo4j employee. Short-slot format, pitch-shaped, plugging the Neo4j startup program at the end.",
    "The load-bearing phrase — \"thin agents on a smarter shared substrate\" — is repeated verbatim as the thesis and delivered as the vocabulary import the audience should walk out with.",
    "Three-pillar architecture: (1) business-facing ontology in human vocabulary, (2) technical ontology of every data source and its schema, (3) runtime execution traces from agents walking the graph. The mapping between (1) and (2) plus the traces from (3) form the substrate.",
    "Explicit target market: Fortune 20 global bank, a large Bay Area tech platform company, and a leading fintech — problem framed as enterprise-scale, not startup-scale.",
    "Notable — cites Swyx / latent space podcast (~1 week prior): \"You cannot vibe code with just markdown files.\" Positioned as validation that skills-as-markdown is part of the solution but not sufficient at scale.",
    "For the ZP corpus: strongly resonant vocabulary at the substrate-abstraction level. Neo4j's substrate is semantic (where does data live, and did the last agent succeed with it); ZeroPoint's is trust (who authorized this act, and can we prove it). Complementary layers, not competing. The phrase \"thin agents on a smarter shared substrate\" is exactly the position ZP is claiming at a different layer."
  ]
